ent of inertia about the ![](images/23/235efd30dbd8ca8883230793c8b79882afd19443c612b54f30b5cd507a646c9c.png)-axis is\n\n![](images/14/148b58f76868ac2f7a85e68627f91f2fa47c6c6a2bd5bcb67af8ba8af011167a.png)" 13194139534572 17592186045417 false] [17592186129030 1074 "For a plane lamina ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) with mass density function ![](images/ac/ac8c3fda52a02808979c7543290abb75797c85b3b0db8aed7a51cc2afda66995.svg) we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis is given by\n![](images/66/66764283bb64f05a967263265ff2c3253049505060577b19b8beb83be225efb9.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis is given by\n![](images/f3/f30602db401e65f6fa3e007153a1e2d8ddd35f5ef202bab4118ba2663ec03325.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n![](images/a6/a6f586b23532d418063be8ede925099a9dd47ba598ca4fab8190c4f7c4889595.svg)\n\nFirst, notice that ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a type I region with representation\n\n![](images/0b/0b668ee2254c04000cdd2a7f9434e780844366cd2f6f4dbd0798840a688d744e.svg)\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/68/68904cbee1839a61f4d65c5ff03066d8f28b8a5e070f6ddb00c82c29cfd93fcd.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is \n![](images/7f/7fa087c9262341fe08582d9053395cc577a1c441e3ca201619e13deda73c8dc3.svg)\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n![](images/4e/4ea37907521bd2d810e88505cf91e44b78cd2400bba8ab67ef244c1cb62bdeaa.svg)\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis is\n\n![](images/ab/ab2f126d6507eb07fb23f834015c981794f3367a2f14b7e43ac688d5a9af803c.svg)" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426166 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426168 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129211 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426169 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426167 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426171 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426172 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129212 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426173 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426161 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426162 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129284 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426170 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426163 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426164 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186426165 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129369 1050 1440.0 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129532 1071 "![](images/64/64cadaa8b079c80659d661be55709e572c05b185a6b6fdb60e24801a9d1e84a5.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y = 2x$ and $y = 3 - x$, as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 false] [17592186129532 1071 "![](images/64/64cadaa8b079c80659d661be55709e572c05b185a6b6fdb60e24801a9d1e84a5.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y=2x$ and $y=3-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129532 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 3-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/bc/bcf045b47856148dc5f7c05a2800804fb34aad2d0755983375c20c35bb00f0e4.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 2=3.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{3-y}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{3-y}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(3-y-\\frac{1}{2}y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{y}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[{y}^{3}-\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}[(8-6)-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129534 1071 "![](images/ff/ff6c3e308d89c9d07cff993f097fe2fcfafb22dab3399b9056f8db167b1b4b80.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y = 2x$ and $y = x^{2}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $λ(x, y) = 6x + 14y$, find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 false] [17592186129534 1071 "![](images/ff/ff6c3e308d89c9d07cff993f097fe2fcfafb22dab3399b9056f8db167b1b4b80.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=6x+14y,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129534 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fb20e4c3052e28aeb2e6dad5054b0458db2d3b23d10991cfae27b5b00655a8fe.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}{x}^{2}(6x+14y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}6{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[6{x}^{3}y+7{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(12{x}^{4}+28{x}^{4})-(6{x}^{5}+7{x}^{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}40{x}^{4}-6{x}^{5}-7{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[8{x}^{5}-{x}^{6}-{x}^{7}]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =256-64-128 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129535 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129535 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $y$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the origin." 13194139534572 17592186045417 false] [17592186129537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129537 1071 "![](images/82/82ed1d9c8965c445488256f2319bd2e1a25b2f685d5ad39c88f2b2ba7f7f5525.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$, shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." 13194139534572 17592186045417 false] [17592186129537 1071 "![](images/82/82ed1d9c8965c445488256f2319bd2e1a25b2f685d5ad39c88f2b2ba7f7f5525.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129537 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(81-0)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129538 1071 "![](images/0a/0a85b0be5f8e5db2cf4d1716e4afe4a2780c500d94a1ce282a9d0044f93ba084.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r = 1$ and $r = \\sqrt{3}$ and the polar rays $θ = \\frac{π}{6}$ and $θ = \\frac{π}{3}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $λ(x, y) = \\frac{6}{x}$. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 false] [17592186129538 1071 "![](images/0a/0a85b0be5f8e5db2cf4d1716e4afe4a2780c500d94a1ce282a9d0044f93ba084.png)\n\nConsider the plate that occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the polar circles $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{3}$ and the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. The mass density function of the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{6}{x}.$ Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186129538 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nAlso, the mass density function in polar coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{6}{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}(\\frac{6}{r}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ){r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}6{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =2{r}^{3}{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}) \\\\  & =(3\\sqrt{3}-1)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}-1) \\\\  & =10-4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186130474 1064 "Let's now find a formula for the moment of inertia of a plane lamina with continuously varying mass density.\n\nConsider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ (in mass/area), which depends on the position.\n\nBy choosing a finite number of points $(x_{ij},y_{ij}),$ where $i=1,2,\\ldots,m$ and $j=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we can break the region $D$ into non-overlapping boxes $B_{ij} = [x_{ij}, x_{ij} + \\Delta x] \\times [y_{ij}, y_{ij} + \\Delta y]$ of area $\\Delta x \\Delta y = \\Delta A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5ac4166ed637b4de525f069673cc6abfaeb2e8e659b3a212d6f2f4d1bf9fc11.png)\n\nFor large $m$ and $n,$ the mass density in each box, $B_{ij}$ is approximately constant and equal to the mass density of a point $(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*)$ in the box. Hence, the mass of this part of the lamina is approximately the product of the mass density at this point and the area of the box:\n\n$$\nm_{ij} \\approx \\lambda(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\Delta A\n$$\n\nSuppose the function $r(x,y)$ gives the distance of each point $(x,y) \\in D$ from a fixed axis $L.$ Then, using the formula for the moment of inertia for a collection of masses, summing over these points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{i}[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2} \\\\  & \\approx {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(\\lambda ({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})\\cdot ΔA)[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2} \\\\  & \\approx {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\lambda ({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2}ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the limit as the length and width of the boxes tend to zero, we get an exact representation of the moment of inertia as an integral:\n\n$$\nI = \\lim\\limits_{m,n\\to \\infty} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^m \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^n \\lambda(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*) [r(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*)]^2 \\Delta A\n= \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about a fixed axis $L$ of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nI_L = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the point $(x,y)$ to the axis $L.$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll limit the axis of rotation to the $x$- and $y$-axes. Note that the distance between a point $(x,y)$ and the $x$ and $y$ coordinate axes are $r_x(x,y) = |y|$ and $r_y(x,y) = |x|,$ respectively.\n\n![](images/8a/8a94fcdb8bb1a97e2bc9122e88ca7bd4f441299d2ce3c7bee819e644cf4eae89.png)\n\nHence, the moments of inertia of a plate with respect to the coordinate axes $x$ and $y,$ denoted $I_x$ and $I_y$, respectively, are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA related concept is the **moment of inertia about the origin** (or **polar moment of inertia**), which deals with the strength of an object when it is rotating. It is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2)\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm dA = I_x + I_y.\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 false] [17592186130474 1064 "Let's now find a formula for the moment of inertia of a plane lamina with continuously varying mass density.\n\nConsider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ (in mass/area), which depends on the position.\n\nBy choosing a finite number of points $(x_{ij},y_{ij}),$ where $i=1,2,\\ldots,m$ and $j=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we can break the region $D$ into non-overlapping boxes $B_{ij} = [x_{ij}, x_{ij} + \\Delta x] \\times [y_{ij}, y_{ij} + \\Delta y]$ of area $\\Delta x \\Delta y = \\Delta A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5ac4166ed637b4de525f069673cc6abfaeb2e8e659b3a212d6f2f4d1bf9fc11.png)\n\nFor large $m$ and $n,$ the mass density in each box, $B_{ij}$ is approximately constant and equal to the mass density of a point $(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*)$ in the box. Hence, the mass of this part of the lamina is approximately the product of the mass density at this point and the area of the box:\n\n$$\nm_{ij} \\approx \\lambda(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\Delta A\n$$\n\nSuppose the function $r(x,y)$ gives the distance of each point $(x,y) \\in D$ from a fixed axis $L.$ Then, using the formula for the moment of inertia for a collection of masses, summing over these points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{i}[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2} \\\\  & ≈{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(\\lambda ({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})\\cdot ΔA)[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2} \\\\  & ≈{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\lambda ({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2}ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the limit as the length and width of the boxes tend to zero, we get an exact representation of the moment of inertia as an integral:\n\n$$\nI = \\lim\\limits_{m,n\\to \\infty} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^m \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^n \\lambda(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*) [r(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*)]^2 \\Delta A\n= \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about a fixed axis $L$ of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nI_L = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the point $(x,y)$ to the axis $L.$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll limit the axis of rotation to the $x$- and $y$-axes. Note that the distance between a point $(x,y)$ and the $x$ and $y$ coordinate axes are $r_x(x,y) = |y|$ and $r_y(x,y) = |x|,$ respectively.\n\n![](images/8a/8a94fcdb8bb1a97e2bc9122e88ca7bd4f441299d2ce3c7bee819e644cf4eae89.png)\n\nHence, the moments of inertia of a plate with respect to the coordinate axes $x$ and $y,$ denoted $I_x$ and $I_y$, respectively, are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA related concept is the **moment of inertia about the origin** (or **polar moment of inertia**), which deals with the strength of an object when it is rotating. It is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2)\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm dA = I_x + I_y.\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186281631 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 4\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186281631 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 4},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." 13194139534572 17592186045417 false] [17592186367839 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the sum of its distance from the \n$x$-axis and its distance from the $y$-axis. Find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186367839 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.$\nThe mass d