426119 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426160 1079 #uuid "fe230e80-9c4a-5f11-8af8-1f23153eddbf" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426155 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426161 1069 "q-163177" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426161 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the $x$-axis.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426161 1072 #uuid "12797033-8f7a-5550-a318-a8461c5f9eb7" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426161 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k{y}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}k{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =k[x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =k(2-0)(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3M}{2}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k{y}^{2}=\\frac{3M}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}({\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x)({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}[x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}(2-0)(\\frac{1}{5}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426157 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426162 1069 "q-163176" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426162 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426162 1072 #uuid "288539c8-32f9-52b9-b8ac-741c3164b236" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426162 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =k(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3M}{2}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=\\frac{3M}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{[\\frac{1}{5}y+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{14}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426154 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426163 1069 "q-163253" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426163 1071 "![](images/74/74ae29ecab0a2d87fb020cc0801277a4a08dd11d38ea2b81f0718c6d30a19598.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ bounded by the polar circles $r=2$ and $r=3,$ as shown above. The mass density at any point is proportional to its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426163 1072 #uuid "40276498-22d3-5917-98ab-3914446aeb38" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426163 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D,$ in polar coordinates, can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}kr\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{2}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{19}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{38\\pi k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr=\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }({\\int }_{2}^{3}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{211}{5}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{633M}{95}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426151 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426164 1069 "q-163250" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426164 1071 "![](images/52/52a326d518bdbe32f119d131e67ae6eb35a3c72479c7ccb5a35dae030d6b9e1c.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the second quadrant bounded by the polar circles $r=1,$ $r=\\sqrt{3},$ and the coordinate axes, as shown above. The mass density at any point is proportional to the square of its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426164 1072 #uuid "648c554b-c03a-5281-b06a-dd87f9becdf0" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426164 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D,$ in polar coordinates, can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=k{r}^{2},$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}k{r}^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=k{r}^{2}=\\frac{M}{\\pi }{r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ){|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{13}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13M}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426156 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426165 1069 "q-163244" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426165 1071 "![](images/cc/ccf2ad2b3d8b39dbacdcdce6bd317c5720ce3b750249f6c328e60d3c74ddb4ef.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the lower half plane bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426165 1072 #uuid "6773c2a6-0930-567e-b7b2-ce599d9b40d9" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426165 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{2M}{\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426150 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426166 1069 "q-163214" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426166 1071 "![](images/75/7545f5c48a86aa8ab7a8f46272e01f3021b7d7be10eea2019ce31c3225ca2f4d.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y=x$ and $y=2-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426166 1072 #uuid "6c69411e-7617-5386-8531-531c78fc6d71" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426166 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b1ced30e4cc1fe984b56707f11e8720f2ee68c5b204b6ea7b8d607a65276acca.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=M.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{y}^{2-y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-y)^{3}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[-\\frac{1}{4}(2-y)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{M}{12}{[(2-y)^{4}+{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{M}{12}(1+1-16-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426160 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426167 1069 "q-163195" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426167 1071 "![](images/1b/1bed15fe8f829f2a2248d5f366e4ca5df84274a767add87b3d26da9d3c2ab12d.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $x$-axis and the lines $y=x$ and $y=4-x$, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=2x,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426167 1072 #uuid "780eb3bd-e477-5b2c-a046-3471aa40cca0" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426167 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fee47e2abe427ce056c77adb5df9a003263bade6e6e3e8d62ca789c162e2348c.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}2x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{x=y}^{x=4-y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(4-y)^{2}-{y}^{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}16{y}^{2}-8{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{16}{3}{y}^{3}-2{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{3}-32 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426153 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426168 1069 "q-163203" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426168 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426168 1072 #uuid "a005e400-255d-52bf-b2df-866a68951503" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426168 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=3\\cdot 2=6.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{6}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{y}^{2}[x]_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}{[{y}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{6}(27-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{13M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426159 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426169 1069 "q-163202" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426169 1071 "A rectangular plate of mass $M\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426169 1072 #uuid "a576d72d-3059-5635-bf8f-38bb7570610e" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426169 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=1\\cdot 3=3.$ \n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{M}{3}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{9}[y]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $\\frac{7M}{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426149 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426170 1069 "q-163190" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426170 1071 "![](images/e8/e818c7ab51d26b642039c4f5e5157dee97772eab41b130ff8c9243f102c5fa20.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the lines $y=3x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. The mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426170 1072 #uuid "c54dac84-cf20-51e9-9f3f-0a76f67b2f7b" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426170 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/b7/b7457bee1577c81fc5c04d4b1a116e82790d7d251dc61bd0611d00aae7d34bfe.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(3x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{[3{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{M}{3}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=\\frac{M}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}(3x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[2{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426158 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426171 1069 "q-163175" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426171 1071 "![](images/eb/eb4ea276f4b876ce42b3701d60c1a5967769b62b328c16e9f9e75ef050f18d6f.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $y$-axis and the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=15x,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426171 1072 #uuid "c8a5bd24-d88b-5487-ab23-00b382428ad5" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426171 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f1/f11f3026c9cc7f315781470791f03d3df97d7f292f8b713a1903f021bd5bf6c6.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}{x}^{2}(15x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}15{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}15{x}^{3}[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}15{x}^{3}(x+2-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}15{x}^{4}+30{x}^{3}-15{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3{x}^{5}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =96+120-160 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426152 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426172 1069 "q-163145" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426172 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426172 1072 #uuid "d5a11202-6be7-5636-bb7a-f06e82bc2539" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426172 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{y}^{2}\\cdot x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{2}^{4}x{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{2}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{6}{5}{y}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}-0 \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis is $1.2\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186426148 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426173 1069 "q-163147" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426173 1071 "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,e\\le x\\le {e}^{3},\\,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}$\nwith mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ where $x$ and $y$ area measured in meters. Find the moment of inertia of this lamina about the $x$-axis." 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426173 1072 #uuid "e2c7c1bb-fa37-5eb7-bb09-c424d91d3ac4" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true] [17592186426173 1074 "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{3}}{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{3}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{e}^{{e}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis is $7.5\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$" 13194139534572 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0028cd5f-1e12-5c9e-a228-965099fee635" 17592186426078 "answer-080d8399-ca6e-5bfc-9948-10e6f55ec587" 17592186426079 "answer-0994a76c-ed5e-593a-894e-4f2204ff3abf" 17592186426080 "answer-192a7bb2-a35e-573a-b092-31cfb1ddac02" 17592186426081 "answer-1a308805-aad8-54a7-b5b1-064462e81b88" 17592186426082 "answer-1ae42d1a-6d46-5f68-a58f-63f3f01ba083" 17592186426083 "answer-1c2f23ac-51be-5a48-810d-c8379ed6f0fe" 17592186426084 "answer-212cb4ac-b452-5d7c-895a-ebccb8fb552a" 17592186426085 "answer-21e7bd91-295b-518a-b561-81121cd85323" 17592186426086 "answer-22b4eab4-e67f-5253-a859-3498612df35b" 17592186426087 "answer-23bbe7e1-a0cc-5c25-ab11-08a6c5e241a9" 17592186426088 "answer-2a8aba61-7fbb-51be-b039-d908378043a6" 17592186426089 "answer-2fe8041f-89cd-55a1-bc49-ec71764fdc11" 17592186426090 "answer-3056ba5d-f7d1-55a3-8c91-5da94f695f90" 17592186426091 "answer-32392a3b-31a7-5a44-8746-b4c85858d143" 17592186426092 "answer-33fd7f69-69b9-5e20-a9bf-e30e6305ddd5" 17592186426093 "answer-383fad8b-59a8-5b43-ad3d-50f07ba39230" 17592186426094 "answer-3b931fa2-83ac-51fe-bb7c-19a20e4185e2" 17592186426095 "answer-3bf55755-4aed-5d41-88a5-9c0a40b76ff2" 17592186426096 "answer-3f77656b-3e45-51e6-975e-6c7b96eab0a0" 17592186426097 "answer-3ff58e4c-ec26-5573-9e2f-2d04c31bdf0d" 17592186426098 "answer-3ff64439-82c0-5c14-a157-cb5ff22cd101" 17592186426099 "answer-4723d509-fb10-5fce-b47c-17128e37c80a" 17592186426100 "answer-4ef1ca64-5df4-54c2-97e4-48430b8722f4" 17592186426101 "answer-52708feb-3913-5690-b2e1-2173e86cf70b" 17592186426102 "answer-591fa420-a919-5ad2-a1d5-c2c1c7db9500" 17592186426103 "answer-68bfb188-a994-53ac-b526-94cd37c66536" 17592186426104 "answer-71f8202f-2dfa-54c8-92d2-f18784e63f6b" 17592186426105 "answer-76eeb049-de78-53bc-bb34-66955cbadcbb" 17592186426106 "answer-775fe094-a717-571b-bd6f-fac3a15f6c76" 17592186426107 "answer-77d105e7-3ddf-576d-b7fd-6524428a349e" 17592186426